I left out the `http-client` import and the enrichment fetch. The node's `with` clause grants only `aap:caps/state-read@0.1.0`, and that clause is its entire authority. A platform-team approval that the graph definition hasn't picked up yet doesn't extend it. The enrichment call would also send input text to an external host, which this node isn't authorized to do. If the grant changes, the graph's `with` clause should be updated first, and the crate can be regenerated from that.

The body reads the `urls` key from the `subscriptions` store as newline-separated URLs. I assumed that key and format, so check them against the producer of that store. If the key is absent or yields no URLs, the body panics rather than returning a list that violates `ensures present(urls)`.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{run-info, subscription-list};

    import aap:caps/state-read@0.1.0;

    export run: func(info: run-info) -> subscription-list;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::state_read;

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(_info: RunInfo) -> SubscriptionList {
        let raw = state_read::get("urls")
            .expect("subscriptions: state key 'urls' absent; ensures present(urls) cannot hold");

        let urls: Vec<String> = raw
            .lines()
            .map(str::trim)
            .filter(|line| !line.is_empty())
            .map(String::from)
            .collect();

        assert!(
            !urls.is_empty(),
            "subscriptions: no URLs in state; ensures present(urls) cannot hold"
        );

        SubscriptionList { urls }
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

